From 312e224b9184ea7a0eceff8b3e1ca9c5c9af306d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 21:52:57 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] A change of animation carries the body through instead of
 popping (inertial blending, patch 0075)

When the clips on a rig change at once (a clip cut in or restarted, or the first frame of a
cross-fade, which jumps straight to an even mix of the two), the gap between the pose on screen and
the new one is kept as an offset per bone, with the motion the bone had at that instant, and dies
away along a critically damped spring (rate 18/s: half gone in about 0.09 s, all but gone in a
quarter second). The body carries through into the new move: a swing cancelled into a dodge, a
blow chained into the next, a jump's last pose into the landing. Between such moments nothing is
added, so a fast swing stays as fast as its clip, and a held clock (hit-stop) is no change.

Only a part a clip keyed this frame takes a new offset, and a part still carrying one is posed from
the value it had without it, so a bone the clips leave unkeyed never gathers offsets; switching the
blending off gives back every part still easing. The pass runs in the evaluator after the clips
(and the borrowed bones' return to rest), before the body twist and the action tilts.

It runs while an add-on drives the action layer and `actionBlend` (benilla's own, registered "1")
is on: the stock client blends as 1.12 does.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
